# Arcade dongle module options.
#
# Every symbol is prefixed with ARCADE_ so this module can live next to
# other dongle modules without fighting over Kconfig names.

config ARCADE_SHELL
    bool "An `arcade` command on the shell"
    depends on SHELL
    default y
    help
      Reads and writes the settings the dongle remembers - the theme, the
      mute, which widget is in which slot - so they can be changed over a
      serial connection instead of by rebuilding.  The theme and the mute
      take effect as you type; the rest are stored and drawn on the next
      boot, because every slot widget allocates its scratch bitmap from the
      slot it was given at init.

      Only the shell backend costs anything: turn CONFIG_SHELL on and this
      comes with it.  On a dongle that means a CDC ACM endpoint over the USB
      it is already plugged into.

config ARCADE_PROFILE_SLOTS
    int "How many profiles the dongle can hold"
    default 5
    range 1 16
    help
      A profile is every setting at once under a name, written to flash so it
      travels with the dongle rather than with the browser that made it.  Each
      one costs about eight hundred bytes of the storage partition when it is
      used, and nothing while the slot is empty.

config ARCADE_ROTATE_DISPLAY
    int "Rotate the display (0, 90, 180 or 270)"
    default 0

config ARCADE_FRAME_INTERVAL
    int "Milliseconds between frames"
    default 33
    help
      33 ms is roughly 30 frames per second.  Raise it for a calmer,
      cheaper animation, lower it for a snappier one.

config ARCADE_SOUND
    bool "Drive the I2S amplifier"
    default y
    select I2S
    help
      Needs a MAX98357A (or anything else that takes I2S straight in) on the
      pins the shield's arcade_amp node names.  With no amplifier in the
      devicetree this does nothing either way.

config ARCADE_SOUND_VOLUME
    int "How loud, 0 to 100"
    default 80
    range 0 100
    help
      100 is unity: one voice at full tilt reaches full scale on its own, and
      a limiter catches whatever stacks on top of it.  80 is the default
      because the last 2 dB above it costs more squashing than it is worth.
      Take the rest from the amplifier: GAIN tied to GND instead of left
      floating is 6 dB and does not touch the waveform.

config ARCADE_SOUND_BASS_FLOOR_HZ
    int "Lowest pitch worth sending to the speaker"
    default 0
    range 0 4000
    help
      A small speaker radiates almost nothing below its own resonance, so a
      note written down there reaches it as silence that has still cost mixing
      headroom.  Notes below this are doubled until they clear it, which keeps
      the intervals - a chord stays that chord, voiced higher.

      The lower of the two chirp notes is D5, at 587 Hz, which most small
      drivers manage - so there is little here for this to fix.  It is kept
      for a score of your own in tools/tunes.py that does go low.

config ARCADE_SOUND_CONNECT
    bool "Chirp when a keyboard connects or drops off"
    default y
    help
      Two chime notes rising when a half reports in, the same shape falling
      when it goes away, so the dongle tells you the link dropped without your
      having to look at it.

      The dongle is the central and ZMK announces the split connection only on
      the peripheral side, so this reads it off the battery level instead: 0
      means gone.  A half sitting at a genuine 0% therefore reads as gone,
      which is the same bargain the battery readout already makes.  Nothing
      sounds at power-on, when the halves connecting is not news.

config ARCADE_MUTE_THRESHOLD
    int "Milliseconds of hold that mute and unmute the speaker"
    default 600
    help
      The third and longest press on the action button, past the short one
      that opens the dashboard and the medium one that changes theme.  Muting
      stops whatever is sounding; unmuting chirps, so you can tell it worked.
      The setting is saved, so a muted dongle comes back muted.

      Set it to 0 to leave the button with its two actions and no mute.

config ARCADE_SOUND_SAMPLE_RATE
    int "Samples per second"
    default 16000
    help
      The nRF I2S clock cannot hit every rate exactly and the driver picks the
      closest it can.  16 kHz is plenty for square waves and keeps the blocks
      small.

config ARCADE_WPM_SPEED
    bool "Speed the game up while you type"
    default y

config ARCADE_WPM_SLOW
    int "Words per minute below which the game idles"
    default 20

config ARCADE_WPM_FAST
    int "Words per minute above which the game runs fast"
    default 60

config ARCADE_DEFAULT_GAME
    string "Which game the panel plays"
    default "pacman"
    help
      "pacman" is the maze, "shooter" is the meteors, "bomber" is the brick
      field, "fighter" is the two of them on a stage, "commando" is the run
      along the ridge, "frogger" is the road and the river, "kong" is the
      girders and "tempest" is the well.  All of them are
      self-playing animations on the same panel and any of them can be chosen
      later from the shell or the configurator, so this is only what a freshly
      flashed dongle comes up on.

